{"date": "2026-10-01", "post_id": 1430516, "title": "Harvest Moon with Erupting Mount Etna", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-1-harvest-moon-with-belt-of-venus-and-erupting-mount-etna/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation: </strong>Have you ever seen the full moon rise? This colorful <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdzPeG_kWNB/?img_index=1\">image</a> was photographed last weekend in <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sicily\">Sicily</a>, just outside the town of <a href=\"https://www.italia.it/en/sicily/enna/nicosia\">Nicosia, in Italy</a>. It is a composite photograph that shows the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/moon/\">Moon</a> rising as the sky turns darker. Lower clouds are reflecting the colors of antitwilight, while ash and gas from <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/the-continuing-eruption-of-mt-etna-1614/\">Mount Etna</a> are seen higher in the background. The pink band lower in the sky is called the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod/apod-2026-august-1-buck-moon-and-belt-of-venus/\">Belt of Venus</a>. During a full moon, the Moon and the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/sun/\">Sun</a> are in opposition in the sky: the moon rises as the sun sets. The <a href=\"https://lco.global/spacebook/sky/moon-phases/\">lunar phase cycle</a> lasts approximately 29.5 days (but the Moon takes approximately 27 days to orbit the Earth). In some cultures of the Northern Hemisphere, the September full moon is called the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-october-9-the-jenga-moon/\">Harvest Moon</a>. <a href=\"https://asterisk.apod.com/discuss_apod.php?date=261001\">Does your culture have a special name for it?</a> (A <a href=\"https://images.unsplash.com/vector-1738312097380-45562da00459\">full moon</a> by any other name would shine as bright.)<br><br><strong>APOD's email for image submissions has changed.</strong> Please see: <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/lib/apsubmit2015.html\">APOD Submissions</a><br><strong>APOD's main NASA site has <a href=\"https://asterisk.apod.com/viewtopic.php?t=45023\">moved</a> </strong>: From <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/\">apod.nasa.gov</a> to <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod\">science.nasa.gov/apod</a><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong>sharpless", "credit": "<a href=\"https://www.dariogiannobile.com/\">Dario Giannobile</a>", "copyright": "<a href=\"https://www.dariogiannobile.com/\">Dario Giannobile</a>", "alt": "Rise of the Harvest Moon over the slopes of Mount Etna and the simultaneous play of colors in the anti-twilight.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-1-harvest-moon-with-belt-of-venus-and-erupting-mount-etna/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Harvest%20Moon%20Trail%20With%20Venus%20Belt%20and%20Erupting%20Mount%20Etna%204%20LD.jpg?w=1600&h=1067&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 October 1 - Harvest Moon with Erupting Mount Etna</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-1-harvest-moon-with-belt-of-venus-and-erupting-mount-etna/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Cecilia Chirenti\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: Have you ever seen the full moon rise? This colorful image was photographed last weekend in Sicily, just outside the town of Nicosia, in Italy. It is a composite photograph that shows the Moon rising as the sky turns darker. Lower clouds are reflecting the colors of antitwilight, while ash and gas from Mount Etna are seen higher in the background. The pink band lower in the sky is called the Belt of Venus. During a full moon, the Moon and the Sun are in opposition in the sky: the moon rises as the sun sets. The lunar phase cycle lasts approximately 29.5 days (but the Moon takes approximately 27 days to orbit the Earth). In some cultures of the Northern Hemisphere, the September full moon is called the Harvest Moon. Does your culture have a special name for it? (A full moon by any other name would shine as bright.)APOD&#039;s email for image submissions has changed. Please see: APOD SubmissionsAPOD&#039;s main NASA site has moved : From apod.nasa.gov to science.nasa.gov/apodTomorrow&#039;s picture: sharpless\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"harvest moon with erupting mount etna\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"Harvest Moon with Erupting Mount Etna\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-1-harvest-moon-with-belt-of-venus-and-erupting-mount-etna/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Harvest%20Moon%20Trail%20With%20Venus%20Belt%20and%20Erupting%20Mount%20Etna%204%20LD.jpg?w=1600&#038;h=1067&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Harvest%20Moon%20Trail%20With%20Venus%20Belt%20and%20Erupting%20Mount%20Etna%204%20LD.jpg?w=1600&#038;h=1067&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"Rise of the Harvest Moon over the slopes of Mount Etna and the simultaneous play of colors in the anti-twilight.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-10-01T00:05:00-04:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-09-30T18:37:32-04:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"Harvest Moon with Erupting Mount Etna\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Harvest%20Moon%20Trail%20With%20Venus%20Belt%20and%20Erupting%20Mount%20Etna%204%20LD.jpg?w=1600&#038;h=1067&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 October 1<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Harvest%20Moon%20Trail%20With%20Venus%20Belt%20and%20Erupting%20Mount%20Etna%204%20LD.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Harvest%20Moon%20Trail%20With%20Venus%20Belt%20and%20Erupting%20Mount%20Etna%204%20LD.jpg\" alt=\"Rise of the Harvest Moon over the slopes of Mount Etna and the simultaneous play of colors in the anti-twilight.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> Harvest Moon with Erupting Mount Etna </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"https://www.dariogiannobile.com/\">Dario Giannobile</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation: </strong>Have you ever seen the full moon rise? This colorful <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdzPeG_kWNB/?img_index=1\">image</a> was photographed last weekend in <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sicily\">Sicily</a>, just outside the town of <a href=\"https://www.italia.it/en/sicily/enna/nicosia\">Nicosia, in Italy</a>. It is a composite photograph that shows the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/moon/\">Moon</a> rising as the sky turns darker. Lower clouds are reflecting the colors of antitwilight, while ash and gas from <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/the-continuing-eruption-of-mt-etna-1614/\">Mount Etna</a> are seen higher in the background. The pink band lower in the sky is called the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod/apod-2026-august-1-buck-moon-and-belt-of-venus/\">Belt of Venus</a>. During a full moon, the Moon and the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/sun/\">Sun</a> are in opposition in the sky: the moon rises as the sun sets. The <a href=\"https://lco.global/spacebook/sky/moon-phases/\">lunar phase cycle</a> lasts approximately 29.5 days (but the Moon takes approximately 27 days to orbit the Earth). In some cultures of the Northern Hemisphere, the September full moon is called the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-october-9-the-jenga-moon/\">Harvest Moon</a>. <a href=\"https://asterisk.apod.com/discuss_apod.php?date=261001\">Does your culture have a special name for it?</a> (A <a href=\"https://images.unsplash.com/vector-1738312097380-45562da00459\">full moon</a> by any other name would shine as bright.)<br><br><strong>APOD's email for image submissions has changed.</strong> Please see: <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/lib/apsubmit2015.html\">APOD Submissions</a><br><strong>APOD's main NASA site has <a href=\"https://asterisk.apod.com/viewtopic.php?t=45023\">moved</a> </strong>: From <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/\">apod.nasa.gov</a> to <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod\">science.nasa.gov/apod</a><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong>sharpless\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-september-30-arp-78-peculiar-galaxy-in-aries/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nCecilia Chirenti, Robert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261001/html"}